how freeing are we really for all my  life i thought that i could be the  orchestrator of my own symphony but if i  work hard on myself and cultivate the  right amount of self-awareness i will be  able to dictate my fate and my future  although i always assumed that free will  is something quite self-evident at some  point i had to question the reality of  this assumption that point was when i  heard my favorite contemporary thinker  sam harris asserting that free will is  an illusion  now i'm standing up here trying to  reason with you  and  you'll think  he looks a little like ben stiller  [Applause]  i was hoping i didn't look that much  like ben stiller  thoughts just emerge in consciousness  okay we are not authoring them  we we can't think them we can't choose  them before we think them that would  require that we think them before we  think them  if you can't control your next thought  and you don't know what it's going to be  until it appears  where is your freedom of will  there's so much to unpack here so let's  start with the fundamentals  understanding what sam harris wants to  say is mind-bending to say the least it  took me hours to decipher his stream of  reasoning and even now i'm not sure if i  understand completely so i will try to  elaborate on what i managed to  understand the basic premise of harry's  postulate is twofold first that we don't  have control over our background the  biochemistry of our brains and also over  the effects of our environment on our  development he writes in his book titled  free will how can we be free as  conscious agents if everything that we  consciously intend is caused by events  in our brain that we do not intend and  of which we are entirely unaware second  that there is substantial evidence that  the brain decides on our actions before  we perform them the famous libid  experiment performed by psychologist  benjamin liebert in the 80s claimed that  activity in the brain's motor cortex can  be detected some 300 milliseconds before  a person feels that they have decided to  move sam harris mentions two more  studies from 2011 in his book and  suggests that these findings are  difficult to reconcile with the sense  that we are the conscious authors of our  actions one fact now seems indisputable  some moments before you are aware of  what you will do next a time in which  you subjectively appear to have complete  freedom to behave however you please  your brain has already determined what  you will do i did my own research and  there are several people disputing the  validity of the libid experiment in any  case this is a debatable topic and  requires further investigation sam  harris essentially suggests that a  powerful mixture of determinism and  randomness shapes the reality of our  experience and since we can't control  determinism and randomness there can be  no such thing as free will  free will and what we call the self are  essentially two sides of the same coin  the self is an illusion the sense of  being an ego an eye a thinker of  thoughts in addition to the thoughts a  an experiencer in addition to the  experience that the sense that we all  have of riding around inside our heads  as a kind of a passenger in the vehicle  of the body that's that's where most  people start when they when they  think about any of these questions most  people don't feel identical to their  bodies they feel like they have bodies  they feel like they're inside the body  and most people feel like they're inside  their heads  now that sense of being a subject a  locus of consciousness inside the head  is an illusion that it makes no  neuroanatomical sense there's no place  in the brain for your ego to be hiding  we know that that everything you  experience your your conscious emotions  and thoughts and  moods and the the impulses that initiate  behavior all of these things are  delivered by by  myriad different processes in the brain  that are spread out over the whole of  the brain that they can be independently  erupted there's we have a changing  system we are a process and there's not  one unitary self that's carried through  from one moment to the next  unchanging and yet we feel that we have  this this self that's just this kind of  center of experience  so since the self is an illusion since  there is no i in the locus of experience  and we are one with consciousness  how can there be free will i think that  we are just arguing semantics here the  notion of free will was introduced as a  way for philosophers to explain the  choices people make and how much control  they have over them as it is stated in  stanford encyclopedia of philosophy from  at least aristotle onwards ancient  philosophers engaged in systematic  reflection of human agency a question  that aristotle seems to recognize while  not satisfactorily answering is whether  the choice an individual makes on any  given occasion is wholly determined by  his internal state he says that the man  is the father of his actions as of  children that is a person's character  shapes how she acts aristotle responds  by contending that her present character  is partly a result of previous choices  she made while this claim is plausible  enough it seems to pass the back since  the man is the father of those earlier  choices and actions too in the course of  history many philosophers tried to  refute the idea of free will and embrace  a more deterministic view of reality  determinism in the west is often  associated with newtonian mechanics and  physics which depicts the physical  matter of the universe as operating  according to a set of fixed noble laws  well you ask one of the deepest  philosophical questions of physics the  question of free will first of all  there's something called newtonian  determinism  newtonian determinism says that the  universe is a clock a gigantic clock  that's wound up at the beginning of time  and it's been ticking ever since  according to newton's laws of motion so  what you're going to eat 10 years from  now on january 1st has already been  fixed it's already known using newton's  laws of motion einstein believed in that  einstein was a determinist and some  people asked einstein well does that  mean that a murderer this horrible mass  murderer isn't really guilty of his  works because it was already preordained  billions of years ago  and einstein said well yeah in some  sense that's true  that even mass murderers were  predetermined but he said they should  still be placed in jail okay  heisenberg then comes along and proposes  the heisenberg uncertainty principle and  says nonsense  there's uncertainty you don't know where  the electron is it could be here here or  many places  simultaneously and this of course  einstein hated because he said god  doesn't play dice with the universe  well  hey get used to it einstein was wrong  god does play dice every time we look at  an electron it moves there's uncertainty  with regards to the position of the  electron so what does that mean for free  will  it means in some sense we do have some  kind of free will in the sense  that no one can determine your future  events given your past history there's  always the wild card there's always the  possibility of uncertainty in whatever  we do so this means that free will  determining the future hey these are  philosophical questions that seem to  indicate that we have some kind of free  will  sam harris writes in his book  losing a belief in free will has not  made me fatalistic in fact it has  increased my feelings of freedom my  hopes fears and neurosis seem less  personal and indelible there's no  telling how much i might change in the  future just as one wouldn't draw a  lasting conclusion about oneself on the  basis of a brief experience of  indigestion one didn't do so on the  basis of how one has thought or behaved  for vast stretches of time in the past a  creative change of inputs to the system  learning new skills forming new  relationships adopting new habits of  attention may radically transform one's  life the point harris tries to make is  also twofold first that our moral  interpretation of others actions should  be conducted under the prism of a  background check we stop hating others  because we think harder about the  origins of their actions and their  psychological and physiological  background we relate differently to  others when we understand them better  second that accepting that there is no  free will allows us to be more relaxed  in the face of absurdity and the  machinations of the cosmos this is  clearly a stoic view letting go of the  agency that free will dictates renders  one more capable of asserting control  over the things one can control  free will isn't free it comes at a cost  if you believe that there is no free  will the ghost is how to accept that the  idea of self doesn't matter anymore that  you just are that you are not the one  pulling the strings anymore you are not  in the cockpit of your experience you  are just floating in your experience but  maybe there is freedom in floating this  is a very liberating feeling for some  but it can also be a very disturbing  feeling for others rejecting free will  shouldn't make you a fatalist evolution  isn't an act of free will it is a part  of the mechanics of the cosmos that  tries to push us towards a certain state  i'm not really sure about the exact  future of that state i can only look at  human history try to recognize patterns  and offer a vague idea of the future  based on those patterns if on the other  hand you believe that there is free will  the cost is how to be a more responsible  human being the freedom to exercise your  will contains also your responsibility  to internalize morality and its  principles and strive for a universal  moral framework that will make  coexistence less arduous personally i  still haven't decided and both ideas as  implausible i understand what sam harris  wants to say that we are an amalgamation  of past experiences biological  mechanisms and environmental influences  and that we don't have control over all  that but we do have control over our  decision to understand all that to some  extent and how to orient ourselves in  the world according to our understanding  of all that we are all essentially part  of a big experiment called life it is  our moral duty to be more like the  scientist and less like the guinea pig  in that experiment  hey guys hope you enjoyed today's video  if you did make sure to like subscribe  turn on notifications and comment below  something cool please so that more  people can discover it  if you want to watch more videos from my  channel check out this one and this one  take care see you soon editor now  you
 

